# Kafka vs RabbitMQ — When to Use Which

Both solve "asynchronous, decoupled communication between services" but
with different underlying models, which makes them fit different jobs.

| | Kafka | RabbitMQ |
|---|---|---|
| **Core model** | Durable, partitioned log | Exchange routes to queues |
| **Message retention** | Configurable (hours-to-forever); consumers can REPLAY history | Consumed messages are typically gone once acked |
| **Ordering** | Guaranteed per-partition (via message key) | Guaranteed per-queue (single consumer) |
| **Routing flexibility** | Simple: topic + partition key | Rich: direct/topic/fanout/headers exchanges |
| **Competing consumers** | Via shared `group-id` | Automatic — just bind multiple consumers to one queue |
| **Broadcast to many services** | Each service uses its own `group-id` | Fanout exchange to multiple queues |
| **Throughput** | Very high (built for high-volume event streams) | High, but generally lower ceiling than Kafka |
| **Dead letter handling** | You implement it (error handler + DLQ topic, as shown) | Built into queue config (`x-dead-letter-exchange`) |
| **Typical use case** | Event streaming, event sourcing, high-volume telemetry/logs, audit trails needing replay | Task queues, RPC-style async messaging, complex routing needs, lower-volume domain events |

## Practical rule of thumb

- **Need to replay/reprocess history, or extremely high throughput (millions of events/sec)?** → Kafka
- **Need flexible routing (route by pattern, broadcast to some-but-not-all consumers, priority queues, delayed messages)?** → RabbitMQ
- **Simple "publish a domain event, a few services react to it" and neither of the above matters much?** → Either works fine; pick whichever your team already operates, since operational maturity matters more than the marginal feature differences for most use cases.

Both integrate identically with the Saga pattern (next topic) - the choreography/orchestration logic doesn't care which broker carries the events.